# STAKEHOLDER MANAGEMENT 

## PURPOSE

The purpose of this stakeholder management plan is to identify all individuals, groups, and organisations that may influence or be affected by the Emergency Department Patient Flow Transformation project.

Effective stakeholder management is critical to successful healthcare transformation because the project involves multiple groups with different priorities, responsibilities, and expectations.

The approach focuses on understanding stakeholder needs, maintaining effective communication, managing resistance to change, and ensuring collaboration throughout the project lifecycle.



# STAKEHOLDER IDENTIFICATION

The project involves stakeholders from clinical, operational, technical, governance, financial, and patient groups.

Each stakeholder has a different level of influence and interest in the project. Understanding these differences allows the project team to develop appropriate engagement strategies and ensure successful delivery.

The key stakeholders involved in this transformation programme include:



## EXECUTIVE SPONSOR

The Executive Sponsor provides strategic leadership, approves major decisions, secures organisational support, and ensures that the project aligns with NHS priorities and organisational objectives.

The sponsor is responsible for removing high-level barriers and supporting the Project Manager when escalation is required.

Engagement Approach:

- Monthly project board meetings
- Executive progress reports
- Benefits and risk reviews
- Strategic decision approvals

---

## PROJECT MANAGER 

The Project Manager is responsible for the overall delivery of the transformation programme.

Responsibilities include:

- Managing project scope, timeline, and budget
- Coordinating project teams
- Managing risks and issues
- Reporting progress
- Ensuring successful project completion

Engagement Approach:

- Weekly project meetings
- Progress reporting
- Risk management reviews
- Stakeholder communication



## CLINICAL LEADS 

Clinical Leads represent doctors, nurses, and healthcare professionals who understand current Emergency Department processes and patient safety requirements.

Their involvement is essential to ensure the solution improves workflows without negatively impacting clinical practice.

Key concerns:

- Patient safety
- Clinical effectiveness
- Impact on healthcare workflows
- Staff adoption

Engagement Approach:

- Clinical workshops
- Process review sessions
- User feedback sessions
- Testing involvement



## EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT STAFF AND NURSING TEAMS

Emergency Department staff are the primary users of the new processes and technology.

The success of the project depends heavily on their acceptance and adoption of the solution.

Potential concerns include:

- Increased workload during transition
- Learning new systems
- Changes to existing processes

Engagement Approach:

- Early involvement in solution design
- Training sessions
- Demonstration sessions
- Feedback collection



## IT DEPARTMENTAL AND DIGITAL TRANSFORMATION TEAM

The IT and Digital Transformation teams are responsible for technical implementation, system integration, cybersecurity considerations, and ongoing support.

Key responsibilities:

- Technical requirements
- System configuration
- Integration with existing systems
- Testing and deployment support

Engagement Approach:

- Weekly technical meetings
- Solution reviews
- Security assessments
- Implementation planning



## INFORMATION GOVERNANCE TEAM

The Information Governance Team ensures that patient information is managed safely and legally.

Their involvement ensures compliance with:

- UK GDPR
- Data Protection Act 2018
- NHS information governance requirements

Key responsibilities:

- Data privacy assessment
- Information security review
- Data handling approval

Engagement Approach:

- Governance reviews
- Privacy impact assessments
- Compliance reporting



## CLINICAL SAFETY OFFICER 

The Clinical Safety Officer ensures that the implementation of the digital solution does not create risks to patient safety.

Responsibilities include:

- Clinical risk assessment
- Safety approval
- Monitoring unintended impacts

Engagement Approach:

- Clinical safety reviews
- Risk assessment meetings
- Implementation approval



## FINANCE AND PROCURMENT TEAMS 

Finance and Procurement teams ensure that the project delivers value for money and follows organisational purchasing procedures.

Responsibilities include:

- Budget monitoring
- Supplier evaluation
- Contract management
- Cost control

Engagement Approach:

- Monthly financial reporting
- Procurement updates
- Budget reviews


## PATIENTS AND SERVICE USERS 

Patients are key stakeholders because the project aims to improve their healthcare experience.

The transformation should result in:

- Reduced waiting times
- Better communication
- Faster access to appropriate care

Engagement Approach:

- Patient feedback surveys
- Experience reviews
- Outcome monitoring



# STAKEHOLDER ENGAGEMENT STRATERGY

## Manage Closely

Stakeholders with high influence and high interest, such as the Executive Sponsor, Clinical Leads, Project Manager, and IT Leadership Team, require regular involvement.

They will be engaged through:

- Project Board meetings
- Progress updates
- Decision-making sessions
- Risk escalation discussions


## Keep Satisfied

Stakeholders such as Finance, Procurement, and senior organisational leaders require regular updates to ensure continued support.

Engagement activities include:

- Budget reports
- Benefit tracking
- Executive summaries


## Keep Informed

Healthcare professionals, administrative teams, and patients require clear communication because they are directly affected by the transformation.

Engagement activities include:

- Training sessions
- Workshops
- Feedback sessions
- Project newsletters


## Monitor

Stakeholders with limited involvement will receive updates when required and will be monitored throughout the project.


# COMMUNICATION APPROACH 

Communication will be managed through a structured approach to ensure stakeholders receive the right information at the right time.

The communication methods include:

- Weekly project team meetings
- Monthly project board reports
- Clinical engagement workshops
- Technical review meetings
- Staff training sessions
- Patient feedback activities

The Project Manager will ensure communication remains transparent, consistent, and aligned with stakeholder needs.


# MANAGING STAKEHOLDER RESISTANCE 

Introducing new technology and processes may create resistance from healthcare professionals.

Common concerns may include:

- "The new system may increase my workload."
- "The existing process already works."
- "I do not have time for additional training."

To manage resistance, the project team will:

- Involve users early in the project
- Explain the reasons for change
- Demonstrate benefits
- Provide appropriate training
- Collect and respond to feedback
- Provide ongoing support after implementation


# STAKEHOLDER RISKS AND MITIGATION

Potential stakeholder-related risks include:

## Resistance from Clinical Teams

Risk:

Healthcare professionals may be reluctant to adopt new workflows.

Mitigation:

Early engagement, user involvement, training, and continuous communication.


## Lack of Executive Support

Risk:

Competing organisational priorities may reduce leadership attention.

Mitigation:

Regular reporting, benefits tracking, and clear escalation processes.


## Data Privacy Concerns

Risk:

Concerns regarding patient information security may delay implementation.

Mitigation:

Early involvement of Information Governance teams, GDPR compliance reviews, and security assessments.


## Low User Adoption

Risk:

Staff may not fully adopt the new system after implementation.

Mitigation:

Comprehensive training, user testing, feedback channels, and post-implementation support.


# STAKEHOLDER SUCCESS MEASURES

Stakeholder engagement will be considered successful when:

- Key stakeholders understand project objectives.
- Clinical teams actively participate in the transformation.
- Users receive appropriate training.
- Communication remains effective.
- Decisions are made collaboratively.
- The solution is successfully adopted.


# CONCLUSION

Effective stakeholder management is essential for successful healthcare transformation.

This project requires collaboration between clinical teams, technology specialists, governance functions, leadership teams, and patients.

By understanding stakeholder expectations, managing resistance, and maintaining strong communication, the project increases the likelihood of successful delivery and sustainable improvements in patient care.
